		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Let's Foosball (table-soccer game for iOS)]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=27220#p27220"><![CDATA[
Here I introduce a new iOS game "Let's Foosball", using Bullet Physics for its core engine.<br>Trailer on iPhone: <a href="http://youtu.be/eId7gjh0Jeo" class="postlink">http://youtu.be/eId7gjh0Jeo</a><br>Trailer on iPad: <a href="http://youtu.be/JOiuZGm3-YY" class="postlink">http://youtu.be/JOiuZGm3-YY</a><br>Full version: <a href="http://itunes.apple.com/us/app/lets-foosball/id480595065?mt=8" class="postlink">http://itunes.apple.com/us/app/lets-foo ... 95065?mt=8</a><br>Free Version: <a href="http://itunes.apple.com/us/app/lets-foosball-free/id496144140?mt=8" class="postlink">http://itunes.apple.com/us/app/lets-foo ... 44140?mt=8</a><br><br>I do 100 SimulateSteps per second by Bullet Physics.<br>Use btSliderConstraint for each rod (all 8 rods).<br>Bullet Physics performance is excellent, so my game could run 50+ FPS on iPad1, and more than 60 FPS on iPad2.<br><div class="inline-attachment"><dl class="file"><dt class="attach-image"><img src="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/download/file.php?id=901" class="postimage" alt="Let&#039;s Foosball Free" onclick="viewableArea(this);" /></dt></dl></div><div class="inline-attachment"><dl class="file"><dt class="attach-image"><img src="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/download/file.php?id=902" class="postimage" alt="Let&#039;s Foosball Free" onclick="viewableArea(this);" /></dt></dl></div><div class="inline-attachment"><dl class="file"><dt class="attach-image"><img src="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/download/file.php?id=903" class="postimage" alt="IMG_0050.PNG" onclick="viewableArea(this);" /></dt></dl></div><p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=9146">macbearchen</a> — Wed Feb 29, 2012 9:38 am</p><hr />
